Open trusts the sequence in a hint-less file's first header whatever its
type byte says. A write that failed or came back short after landing part
of a fresh active file's first record leaves the low bytes of its
sequence and zeros, which read as a sequence below an older file's, and
every open after refuses the database (#471). Reverts the fix.
It takes a write that lands fewer bytes than the sequence needs, into a
file's first record, and a kill before resume(); the Elle chaos run that
found it caught it once in about 190 episodes, so it is not held to a
budget.
Expected: caught (rare).
Guarded-by: "DB recovery: a hint-less file's tail is truncated only in the newest file"

diff --git a/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp b/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
index 7102938..71cb7f4 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
+++ b/bytecaskdb/db_recovery.cpp
@@ -298,9 +298,6 @@ auto first_sequence(const DataFile &file) -> std::uint64_t {
   std::array<std::byte, kHeaderSize> hdr{};
   if (file.read_raw(0, hdr) < kHeaderSize) return 0;
   const auto h = read_header(hdr);
-  if (!is_known_entry_type(h.entry_type) &&
-      tail_is_zero(file, kEntryTypeOffset))
-    return 0;
   return h.sequence;
 }
 
